        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Basement Wall Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Basement wall sealers work well against dampness and vapour and poorly against water under pressure. Most of the failures we hear about are that distinction being ignored.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Walls That Have Active Running Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Waterproofing paint is rated against a few pounds of hydrostatic pressure at best. Where water is actively entering through a crack or the wall-floor joint, a coating will bubble and peel off in sheets. Stop the water at its source — grading, gutters, or a drainage system — before any sealer goes on the wall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Over Efflorescence or Old Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The white powder on a basement wall is salt left behind by evaporating water, and nothing sticks to it. Neither does new sealer stick reliably to old latex paint. Wire-brush the efflorescence off, wash with a masonry cleaner, and strip or thoroughly abrade previous coatings first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Brushing It On Like Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Masonry waterproofers work by filling the pores of the block, which takes a heavy, worked-in coat from a stiff brush rather than a smooth rolled film. Rolled on thin, the coating skips the pinholes that are exactly where water comes through. Two brushed coats, worked into the surface, is the method.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Walls and Ignoring the Floor Joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The joint where the wall meets the slab is the single most common entry point in a basement, and wall sealer does not address it. Coating the walls while leaving that joint open produces a basement that still gets wet, just with tidier walls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">What is the difference between DRYLOK and Flex Seal for basements?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">DRYLOK is a masonry waterproofing paint designed specifically for concrete and block walls, providing a smooth, paintable finish with tested hydrostatic pressure ratings (10-15 PSI). Flex Seal is a liquid rubber coating that creates a flexible membrane — better for sealing cracks and irregular surfaces but more expensive for large areas and doesn't provide the same clean finish. DRYLOK is better for full-wall waterproofing; Flex Seal is better for targeted crack and joint repair. Many professionals recommend using both: Flex Seal on cracks first, then DRYLOK on full walls.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

